WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.360
Every new telescope put up in space has always&nbsp;
found something they didn't expect—something new.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.400
The James Webb Space Telescope is the biggest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.520
most complex telescope that NASA&nbsp;
has ever launched into space.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.880
Telescopes are time machines.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.920
Webb will push us back into the realm of the&nbsp;
first stars and galaxies in the universe.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.120
The Webb telescope is designed to see as far back&nbsp;
in time as we can possibly see luminous objects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.920
when the universe was maybe 100,000,000 years old.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.120
We have a suite of very powerful instruments&nbsp;
to study the atmospheres of planets.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.840
Webb will be very helpful to us in&nbsp;
the search for life in the future.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.880
Up until Hubble flew, we were using telescopes&nbsp;
on the ground, and they couldn't see very far.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.440
[NEWSCAST] 6, 5, 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:27.440
3, 2, 1 and liftoff on the Hubble Space&nbsp;
Telescope, our window on the universe.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.600
When Hubble flew, we knew that it was going to&nbsp;
be able to reach out further into the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.840
but we didn't really have&nbsp;
any idea what was out there.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.160
The Hubble Deep Field image was&nbsp;
the discovery image of the '90s.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.280
What was unique about this&nbsp;
was the idea of spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.800
days and days of valuable Hubble time staring at&nbsp;
a tiny spot in the sky where there was nothing.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:02.800
But when we did a deep image like this,&nbsp;
astronomers suddenly saw that there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:08.560
a richness of galaxies in there. Thousands and&nbsp;
thousands of galaxies we had never seen before.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.040
Basically a history of the&nbsp;
whole universe in one image.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:20.960
The universe is 13,800 million years old.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.480
Light travels very rapidly&nbsp;
but the distances are so huge.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:36.880
Hubble can see in the visible and the ultraviolet&nbsp;
with a little in the infrared. But the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.680
we want to see now, the first stars that turned&nbsp;
on in the universe or the first galaxies, // they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:48.160
were probably very bright objects that emitted&nbsp;
a lot of ultraviolet light. But that ultraviolet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.920
light with a very short wavelength has been&nbsp;
traveling through a universe that's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.520
expanding for about 13.5 billion years. That short&nbsp;
wavelength, as it traverses through all that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.480
gets stretched out so that by the time it reaches&nbsp;
us, it's no longer ultraviolet—it's infrared.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.520
After they had Hubble Deep Field,&nbsp;
they said, "We can't see far enough."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.000
So we need a bigger telescope that's&nbsp;
more sensitive and that can look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.840
longer infrared wavelengths so that&nbsp;
we can see the more distant universe.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:26.240
About 13.7 billion years ago, there were&nbsp;
no stars in the universe. There was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.032
gas and dark matter spread&nbsp;
throughout the universe.

00:03:28.032 --> 00:03:29.280
When the first stars and galaxies&nbsp;
burst forth, at that point we'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.560
gone through nearly 100 to 200&nbsp;
million years of dark ages.&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:42.720
Suddenly, galaxies and stars started popping&nbsp;
up all over the place. A cosmic sunrise. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:49.200
what JWST is for, mapping the first billion years&nbsp;
of galaxy formation and growth in the universe.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.760
The Webb Telescope is the first general purpose,&nbsp;
powerful infrared telescope we've ever had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.080
The Webb Telescope opens up what I&nbsp;
call the infrared treasure chest. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.000
you were a bumble bee at the distance&nbsp;
of the moon away from the telescope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.240
we would be able to pick up&nbsp;
the heat that you send out.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.640
Infrared is special for astronomers because&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.720
cooler objects that can not glow and emit&nbsp;
visible light are able to emit infrared.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:24.240
When you look at the universe, you see&nbsp;
there are lots of sort of dusty, dark areas.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.320
But one of the great things about the infrared&nbsp;
is that light can travel through the dust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.560
the dust becomes almost transparent.&nbsp;
So if we're trying to see how stars and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.840
planets form in their early times,&nbsp;
the infrared is the place to be.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:47.680
If you want to see dim stars, you don't stay&nbsp;
in the city, you go out to the country, right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:53.600
you get away from the lights. We want to see&nbsp;
the // dimmest galaxies there are to see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.520
The earth is pretty bright in the infrared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.720
so you want to get away from the earth.&nbsp;
You want to get away from the moon.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.800
Webb needed to be far away from Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.320
and it needed to be very cold if&nbsp;
it's going to work in the infrared.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.280
If you cool the whole telescope&nbsp;
down near the temperature of space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:19.600
then you can see out with incredible clarity,&nbsp;
over the first billion years of the universe.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.440
So we're going to a point called the Sun-Earth&nbsp;
L2 point. It's a million miles away from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.600
earth on the opposite side of the sun. And&nbsp;
it's a place where the gravity of the sun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:36.400
and the gravity of the earth combine to make a&nbsp;
relatively stable point that follows the earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:43.520
around the sun every 365 days. So if we go out&nbsp;
there and we put up a sun shield, a big umbrella,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.720
from that location, we can block out the sun,&nbsp;
the earth, and the moon at the same time.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.600
But this is obviously no ordinary umbrella.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:01.440
The five individual layers are made of an almost&nbsp;
plastic-like material called Kapton. 200,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.400
watts of solar radiation strikes the hot side,&nbsp;
and the sun shield will only allow about 0.02&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:14.720
watts to get through. If it were suntan lotion, it&nbsp;
would have a solar protection factor of about 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:24.240
million. So the telescope and the instruments&nbsp;
are exposed to cold space and nothing else.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.080
The Webb Telescope is the most difficult&nbsp;
scientific project we've ever undertaken.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.400
It's got to unfold itself in space.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:34.560
James Webb is obviously not going&nbsp;
to fit on the top of a rocket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.520
Something that's that big has to be folded.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:47.840
A consequence of this folding up is that we&nbsp;
have to be able to do the deployment in orbit.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:55.760
The unfolding starts very rapidly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:00.800
Once we're above the atmosphere&nbsp;
the fairing drops away. Then Webb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:04.960
at that point is shooting towards&nbsp;
the moon and out to its final orbit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:15.520
The solar panels have to come out very quickly to&nbsp;
power it. Over the next 29 days, there's a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:21.200
sequence of activities that happens. Antennas,&nbsp;
and particularly the very large sun shield has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:29.840
to be pulled out and be separated. And that&nbsp;
is an amazingly intricate series of steps.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:34.080
Unlike Hubble, we are not orbiting&nbsp;
the Earth. We are going a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:38.080
miles out where no man has gone before&nbsp;
so servicing is not an option for us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:44.400
All the release mechanisms have to work.&nbsp;
If they don't deploy, we've had a bad day.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.520
We'll allow the telescope to cool down&nbsp;
and the instruments to cool down and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.040
we start focusing them. That takes, all&nbsp;
together, six months to get all of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.800
set up. And then we're pretty ready to&nbsp;
say, "Let's try some science projects."

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:02.800
Webb Telescope has four instruments all together.&nbsp;
One is near infrared camera. It's the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:08.960
sensitive of the instruments. Number two is a near&nbsp;
infrared spectrograph, and that one spreads out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:14.000
the light of the stars or galaxies into our&nbsp;
spectrum. And the special invention that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.560
made for that one was the micro shutter array,&nbsp;
designed to measure 100 separate galaxies at once.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:26.080
The third instrument is the mid-infrared&nbsp;
instrument, or MIRI. That's a special one because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.000
it picks up wavelengths where the molecular&nbsp;
emissions are the strongest and also it's for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.000
the coldest objects out there. So we&nbsp;
see new stars that are just forming.

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:42.000
The fourth instrument is the near-infrared&nbsp;
imaging slitless spectrograph and it is used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.400
for extremely high angular resolution&nbsp;
images that might have planets in them.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.520
On average, every star in the galaxy has at&nbsp;
least one planet. So when you look up at the sky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:59.360
the pinpoints of light you see are&nbsp;
planetary systems, not just stars.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:04.720
We are super excited about the&nbsp;
launch of the James Webb Space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:08.000
Telescope because it's going to&nbsp;
bring us new data about planets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:13.840
and my mom and I are going to be working&nbsp;
together on disentangling that data.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.040
My mom is discovering new planets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:23.840
and I take those planets and I try and&nbsp;
understand what their atmospheres are like.&nbsp;

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:34.320
Most planets that have been discovered were noted&nbsp;
because we observed something about the star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:38.080
that indicated the presence of&nbsp;
a planet in orbit around it.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:43.200
You're measuring the brightness&nbsp;
of a star and you're waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.720
for the momentary dimming of light that&nbsp;
happens if a planet in its orbit around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:52.960
the star happens to pass directly&nbsp;
between the star and the telescope.

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:08.320
We discover evidence of a planet buried in&nbsp;
data and that can feel like an abstraction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:12.520
We have to pause and remember that&nbsp;
these places really exist. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:17.840
some place there's a lava ocean&nbsp;
rising and falling with the tide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:25.600
some place there's an Eastern horizon&nbsp;
where two stars are rising, not one.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:30.240
We've only really been able to&nbsp;
scratch the surface in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.320
of what we know about those planets. But&nbsp;
now, with the James Webb Space Telescope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:38.160
we'll start to dig deeper and understand&nbsp;
more about what their atmospheres are like.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:42.640
By looking at the atmosphere&nbsp;
itself you can actually get an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:48.080
idea of what the climate is like and you&nbsp;
get an idea of what the full chemical makeup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:53.840
of the planet is. We want to study&nbsp;
those atmospheres because we think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:59.920
there could be potential signs of&nbsp;
life encoded in those atmospheres.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:05.200
Here on Earth, we have a very specific chemical&nbsp;
makeup in our atmosphere that allows life to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:10.960
flourish. Geology produces both carbon dioxide&nbsp;
and methane, but life produces methane in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.840
100-times-higher quantities. We have more methane&nbsp;
than you would expect on Earth because of life. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:19.040
we can look at this delicate balance&nbsp;
between CO2 and methane on other planets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:22.720
that could give us some indication of&nbsp;
life in the atmospheres of other planets.

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:27.840
One of the most exciting planets that JWST&nbsp;
is going to look at is the TRAPPIST-1 system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:32.480
This is a star that's just about the&nbsp;
size of Jupiter, so it's very small.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:38.560
And it has seven Earth-sized planets&nbsp;
orbiting around the star. And three of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:42.301
those planets in the TRAPPIST-1 system could&nbsp;
be potentially habitable. They've received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.301 --> 00:11:44.880
just the right amount of energy that they could&nbsp;
have surface liquid water potentially on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:50.560
surface.Looking at those planet atmospheres&nbsp;
is going to be like this perfect laboratory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:56.320
of science, where we can sample the&nbsp;
atmospheres of seven different planets.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:01.840
Potentially habitable Earth-sized planets&nbsp;
are relatively common. We expect there to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:05.280
maybe a dozen billion such&nbsp;
worlds in our galaxy alone.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:12.160
Earth didn't make its appearance until billions&nbsp;
of years after the Milky Way formed. But there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:16.480
are planets that formed in those very&nbsp;
earliest moments of the Milky Way. Imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:23.840
how life could evolve on a planet given&nbsp;
10-12 billions of years of evolution.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:38.320
We have lots of reasons to be skeptical of our&nbsp;
understanding of the very early universe. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:43.280
have a pretty good story called the Standard Model&nbsp;
of the universe, which is cosmic dark matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:47.920
cosmic dark energy, ordinary matter, and gravity&nbsp;
pulling things together after the Big Bang.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:51.920
But we've gotten trouble. People don't&nbsp;
have any explanation for where did the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:56.000
dark matter or the dark energy come from?&nbsp;
And we don't know that that's the only kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:57.611
of stuff out there. So recently we're getting&nbsp;
some evidence that maybe there's more than one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.611 --> 00:12:58.480
kind of dark matter or more than one kind&nbsp;
of dark energy. So I think we could get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:00.560
big surprise way out there in the early universe.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:00.775
Every time astronomers build a powerful new tool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.775 --> 00:13:01.089
we discover something new. Nature has&nbsp;
more imagination than humans have.

00:13:01.089 --> 00:13:01.416
What makes us human is to be curious&nbsp;
and to push boundaries and explore and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.416 --> 00:13:01.790
increase our understanding of the universe,&nbsp;
and JWST is the next piece of that puzzle.

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:02.160
The Webb telescope is so powerful, you can't&nbsp;
you can't predict what it's going to see.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:06.480
I'm hoping in my heart that James Webb&nbsp;
finds a whole new field to investigate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.600
Even though I'm 62 years old,&nbsp;
maybe I can go back to grad school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:14.000
get my PhD on something in a brand new&nbsp;
field that was started by James Webb.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:44.720
We are learning about our galaxy&nbsp;
at a very rapid pace. We've gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:49.040
in just a couple decades from finding the&nbsp;
very first planet orbiting another star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:53.520
to having a comprehensive, specific&nbsp;
plan for identifying living worlds.

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:59.360
I really hope that Webb helps me to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:04.560
what fraction of the smallish planets orbiting&nbsp;
the Goldilocks zone are true Earth analogs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:09.520
I feel confident that with the James&nbsp;
Webb Space Telescope, my grandchildren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:14.000
will be able to look up in the sky and&nbsp;
point to a star and say, "There's life."

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:22.320
I think that's going to be a moment that is maybe&nbsp;
even more profound than our Copernican moment that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:27.440
took Earth out of the center of the universe,&nbsp;
because it's going to put an end to cosmic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:43.840
loneliness. It's going to fundamentally change&nbsp;
what we see when we look up in the night sky.

